<HelpTab>
    <FluentText>Select <strong>Settings</strong> on a kiosk to open its settings. Each tab saves on its own.</FluentText>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>General</strong>: the <strong>Client Name</strong>, and its <strong>Location</strong>. The location
            decides which music schedule it follows, which audio clips and schedules play on it, and which
            opening hours apply. Set it on every kiosk. <strong>Party room (optional)</strong> is the party room the kiosk's screen
            is in, typed as it is in a booking's Room or table, or picked from the rooms already used. Party live text on its
            screen then shows that room's party. Leave it blank to show the location's party.</li>
        <li><strong>Health</strong>: version, uptime, memory, disk space, audio and DMX devices, projection windows and
            offline readiness.</li>
        <li><strong>Projection</strong>: which program plays on which display, full screen or borderless, and its size.
            Use <strong>Add Projection</strong> to add one. <strong>Identify displays</strong> at the top shows which
            screen is which (see below).</li>
        <li><strong>Offline</strong>: turn on <strong>Keep screens playing when the server can't be reached</strong>. The kiosk
            keeps a copy of its projection programs and videos and plays them if it loses the server or internet.
            Set how long to wait before switching to offline, and the video cache limit.</li>
        <li><strong>Music</strong>: the <strong>Default Audio Output</strong>. The kiosk checks every minute and switches back
            to it, so a TV or projector that reconnects can't take over the sound. Also the music cache limit, and the
            <strong>Equaliser</strong>: Bass, Mid and Treble sliders and presets for this kiosk's music, saved with
            <strong>Save</strong>. Staff can change the same equaliser live from the <strong>EQ</strong> button on the
            Music page.</li>
        <li><strong>DMX</strong>: which DMX device the kiosk sends lighting to.</li>
        <li><strong>Zone Scoreboard</strong>: the network settings for reading the laser game's scores. The
            <strong>Source IP</strong> is the laser game control box, and the <strong>Destination IP</strong> is where it
            sends to, usually a broadcast address like 192.168.0.255. Pick the network card that's plugged into the
            game's network.
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Only read laser game traffic (capture filter)</strong>: the network card throws away
                    everything except the game's messages, so a busy network uses far less of the kiosk's processor.
                    A connected kiosk applies it straight away. Turn it on, play a game and check the scoreboard
                    still updates. If it doesn't, turn it off again.</li>
                <li><strong>Read traffic sent to other machines (promiscuous mode)</strong>: only needed if the
                    control box sends to a different machine rather than a broadcast address. Leave it on unless
                    you've checked a game works with it off.</li>
                <li>With the capture filter on, the kiosk self-test can only see game messages, so between games it
                    shows a warning rather than "no network traffic".</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Auto Restart</strong>: restart the PC on a schedule (every so many days or weeks, or monthly, at a set time).</li>
        <li><strong>Updates</strong>: which version the PC runs (see the next tab).</li>
    </ul>

    <HelpSection Title="Identify displays">
        <FluentText>
            Lanyard numbers a kiosk's screens Screen 1, Screen 2 and so on, in the order Windows lists them. That isn't
            always left to right, so use <strong>Identify displays</strong> to see which number is which physical screen.
            It's the # button next to Self-test on the Clients page, and a button at the top of the
            <strong>Projection</strong> tab here, in <strong>Add Projection</strong>, in the <strong>Screens</strong>
            window, and beside every <strong>Screen</strong> picker in Automation and dashboard widgets.
        </FluentText>
        <ul>
            <li>For 8 seconds, every screen on the kiosk shows a large number with its name (such as \\.\DISPLAY2), its
                resolution, whether it's the main screen, and the program set up for it or playing on it. Press the
                button again to show the numbers again.</li>
            <li>Whatever is playing carries on underneath, and the numbers can't be clicked or closed at the kiosk.
                They show even during a game, so customers may see them.</li>
            <li>On the Projection tab the same details are listed under the button, so you can match them up without
                standing at the kiosk.</li>
            <li>If a screen is marked <strong>Changed</strong>, Windows has renumbered the screens since the kiosk last
                reported them, usually after a screen was unplugged or swapped. Anything set up for that number now plays
                on the new screen, so check its projection is still right.</li>
            <li>It only works while the kiosk is connected, and needs Lanyard Client 1.0.49 or later. An older kiosk says
                so; use <strong>Restart</strong>, then <strong>Restart Lanyard Client</strong>, to update it.</li>
            <li>The kiosk self-test also lists the screens it can see, under <strong>Screens</strong>.</li>
        </ul>
    </HelpSection>
</HelpTab>
